WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.720
<v Tutor>Good morning, and welcome to Part Two Daily.

2
00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:09.207
<v Tutor>It's Saturday the twenty-first of November, one hundred and nine days until the written paper on the tenth of March.

3
00:00:09.207 --> 00:00:11.195
<v Tutor>Saturday is the spiral review.

4
00:00:11.195 --> 00:00:19.617
<v Tutor>We go back to topics from one, three and six weeks ago, rebuild the answer that earns the marks, then make each one harder by tying it to another system.

5
00:00:19.617 --> 00:00:23.802
<v Tutor>That's how the College writes its questions, and it's how memory sticks.

6
00:00:23.802 --> 00:00:25.267
<v Tutor>Four revisits today.

7
00:00:25.267 --> 00:00:31.859
<v Tutor>First, the central venous waveform and the passive leg raise, with a right ventricle in trouble after cardiac surgery.

8
00:00:31.859 --> 00:00:36.933
<v Tutor>Second, the standardised mortality ratio, in a unit with a big cardiac surgical case mix.

9
00:00:36.933 --> 00:00:41.118
<v Tutor>Third, pyroglutamic acidosis, with a second acid hiding in the same gas.

10
00:00:41.118 --> 00:00:47.134
<v Tutor>Fourth, myxoedema coma in a woman on amiodarone, with the story of a young doctor in Newcastle and a sheep.

11
00:00:47.134 --> 00:00:53.569
<v Tutor>Then a journal watch with two new trials, one on dialysis modality and one on dapagliflozin before cardiac surgery.

12
00:00:53.569 --> 00:00:58.800
<v Tutor>Then a syllabus round-up of six items, from respiratory monitoring to chronic liver failure.

13
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.110
<v Tutor>Picture the second night after a mitral valve repair.

14
00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:08.071
<v Tutor>The patient is ventilated, in new atrial fibrillation at a hundred and twenty, on noradrenaline.

15
00:01:08.071 --> 00:01:12.222
<v Tutor>The central venous pressure reads eighteen millimetres of mercury and climbing.

16
00:01:12.222 --> 00:01:14.654
<v Tutor>The registrar asks whether to give a bolus.

17
00:01:14.654 --> 00:01:17.040
<v Tutor>Does that number tell you anything useful?

18
00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.280
<v Tutor>Hold that thought.

19
00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.990
<v Tutor>This is the 2021 first sitting, question twenty-one.

20
00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:25.300
<v Tutor>It came up in your set on the fifteenth of November.

21
00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:30.156
<v Tutor>Part a: List four patient factors that determine central venous pressure, C V P.

22
00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:31.921
<v Tutor>Twenty percent of marks.

23
00:01:31.921 --> 00:01:42.790
<v Tutor>Part b: List four clinical conditions that may be detected from an abnormal central venous waveform in a euvolaemic patient and for each condition describe the associated waveform features.

24
00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:44.555
<v Tutor>Twenty percent of marks.

25
00:01:44.555 --> 00:01:53.989
<v Tutor>Part c: Explain how one performs and interprets a passive leg raise manoeuvre including its physiological basis, reliability, and limitations in clinical practice.

26
00:01:53.989 --> 00:01:55.700
<v Tutor>Sixty percent of marks.

27
00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:03.920
<v Tutor>The determinants of central venous pressure turned up as a question of their own in 2014, so this is a recurring theme rather than a one-off.

28
00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.018
<v Tutor>Let's rebuild the answer.

29
00:02:06.018 --> 00:02:07.819
<v Tutor>Notice the weighting first.

30
00:02:07.819 --> 00:02:12.707
<v Tutor>Sixty percent sits on the passive leg raise, so most of your ten minutes belongs there.

31
00:02:12.707 --> 00:02:14.816
<v Tutor>Parts a and b are list questions.

32
00:02:14.816 --> 00:02:17.440
<v Tutor>Give four items each, crisply, and move on.

33
00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.207
<v Tutor>For part a, the College wants factors, not a lecture.

34
00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:24.132
<v Tutor>Think of it as pressure at the end of a tube.

35
00:02:24.132 --> 00:02:30.479
<v Tutor>One: intravascular volume and how it's distributed between the stressed and unstressed venous compartments.

36
00:02:30.479 --> 00:02:33.680
<v Tutor>Two: venous tone and right ventricular compliance.

37
00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:38.372
<v Tutor>Three: right ventricular systolic function and pulmonary vascular resistance.

38
00:02:38.372 --> 00:02:41.518
<v Tutor>Four: the tricuspid valve and the cardiac rhythm.

39
00:02:41.518 --> 00:02:49.576
<v Tutor>And five, if you have the space, intrathoracic pressure, which means positive end-expiratory pressure, and the transducer reference level.

40
00:02:49.576 --> 00:02:59.400
<v Tutor>A transducer ten centimetres too low reads about seven millimetres of mercury too high, because ten centimetres of water is about seven point four millimetres of mercury.

41
00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:04.612
<v Tutor>For part b, describe the waveform for each condition, as the question asks.

42
00:03:04.612 --> 00:03:06.583
<v Tutor>Remember the normal shape.

43
00:03:06.583 --> 00:03:08.959
<v Tutor>The a wave is atrial contraction.

44
00:03:08.959 --> 00:03:13.539
<v Tutor>The c wave is the tricuspid valve bulging back at the start of systole.

45
00:03:13.539 --> 00:03:16.032
<v Tutor>The x descent is atrial relaxation.

46
00:03:16.032 --> 00:03:19.510
<v Tutor>The v wave is atrial filling against a closed valve.

47
00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:23.394
<v Tutor>The y descent is the valve opening and the atrium emptying.

48
00:03:23.394 --> 00:03:25.249
<v Tutor>Now the four conditions.

49
00:03:25.249 --> 00:03:29.771
<v Tutor>Atrial fibrillation: no a wave, and the c wave becomes more prominent.

50
00:03:29.771 --> 00:03:37.539
<v Tutor>Complete heart block, or any atrioventricular dissociation: cannon a waves, where the atrium contracts against a closed valve.

51
00:03:37.539 --> 00:03:42.467
<v Tutor>Tricuspid regurgitation: a giant fused c v wave that wipes out the x descent.

52
00:03:42.467 --> 00:03:49.423
<v Tutor>Tamponade: a blunted or absent y descent, because the stiff pericardial space stops the ventricle filling early.

53
00:03:49.423 --> 00:03:55.800
<v Tutor>Constriction is the contrast case: a steep x and a steep y, so the trace looks like the letter M or W.

54
00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:57.602
<v Tutor>Here's a quick one.

55
00:03:57.602 --> 00:03:58.952
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

56
00:03:58.952 --> 00:04:02.120
<v Tutor>In cardiac tamponade, which descent is lost, and why?

57
00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:08.658
<v Tutor>The y descent.

58
00:04:08.658 --> 00:04:13.833
<v Tutor>Early diastolic filling is blocked because the total volume inside the pericardium is fixed.

59
00:04:13.833 --> 00:04:17.766
<v Tutor>The x descent is preserved, because ventricular ejection makes room.

60
00:04:17.766 --> 00:04:23.200
<v Tutor>In constriction, both descents are steep, because early filling is rapid and then stops abruptly.

61
00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:25.169
<v Tutor>Now the sixty percent.

62
00:04:25.169 --> 00:04:26.267
<v Tutor>How to do it.

63
00:04:26.267 --> 00:04:29.822
<v Tutor>Start with the patient semi-recumbent at forty-five degrees.

64
00:04:29.822 --> 00:04:34.109
<v Tutor>Measure baseline stroke volume or cardiac output with a real-time monitor.

65
00:04:34.109 --> 00:04:39.286
<v Tutor>Then tilt the whole bed so the trunk is flat and the legs are raised to forty-five degrees.

66
00:04:39.286 --> 00:04:44.357
<v Tutor>Don't lift the legs by hand, because pain and sympathetic activation confound the result.

67
00:04:44.357 --> 00:04:48.906
<v Tutor>Measure again within the first sixty to ninety seconds, while the effect peaks.

68
00:04:48.906 --> 00:04:52.200
<v Tutor>Then return to baseline and check the value comes back.

69
00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:53.805
<v Tutor>Interpretation.

70
00:04:53.805 --> 00:04:59.620
<v Tutor>A rise in stroke volume or cardiac output of ten percent or more means the patient is fluid responsive.

71
00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:06.692
<v Tutor>The physiology is that you shift a few hundred millilitres of venous blood from the legs and the splanchnic bed into the chest.

72
00:05:06.692 --> 00:05:08.998
<v Tutor>That's a reversible fluid challenge.

73
00:05:08.998 --> 00:05:12.560
<v Tutor>It costs nothing, and it disappears when you lower the legs.

74
00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:14.037
<v Tutor>Reliability.

75
00:05:14.037 --> 00:05:19.908
<v Tutor>The 2016 meta-analysis by Monnet and colleagues in Intensive Care Medicine pooled twenty-one studies.

76
00:05:19.908 --> 00:05:30.788
<v Tutor>A passive leg raise judged on cardiac output had a pooled sensitivity of about eighty-five percent and a specificity of about ninety-one percent, with an area under the curve of point nine five.

77
00:05:30.788 --> 00:05:33.858
<v Tutor>The best threshold was around a ten percent rise.

78
00:05:33.858 --> 00:05:38.275
<v Tutor>When the change was judged on pulse pressure alone, accuracy fell clearly.

79
00:05:38.275 --> 00:05:44.200
<v Tutor>That's the point you missed on fluid responsiveness earlier this month, so say it plainly in the exam.

80
00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:47.216
<v Tutor>The leg raise needs a real-time measure of flow.

81
00:05:47.216 --> 00:05:49.640
<v Tutor>Arterial pressure alone isn't enough.

82
00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:52.380
<v Tutor>What counts as a real-time measure?

83
00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:59.727
<v Tutor>Pulse contour cardiac output, the velocity time integral at the left ventricular outflow tract on echo, or oesophageal Doppler.

84
00:05:59.727 --> 00:06:04.135
<v Tutor>A useful alternative in a ventilated patient is end-tidal carbon dioxide.

85
00:06:04.135 --> 00:06:10.720
<v Tutor>With fixed ventilation, a rise of about five percent during the leg raise suggests that cardiac output has risen.

86
00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:13.505
<v Tutor>Limitations, which carry real marks.

87
00:06:13.505 --> 00:06:17.517
<v Tutor>It needs a bed that tilts and two intact legs and a stable pelvis.

88
00:06:17.517 --> 00:06:22.288
<v Tutor>It's unsuitable with raised intracranial pressure, because lying flat raises it.

89
00:06:22.288 --> 00:06:27.601
<v Tutor>Intra-abdominal hypertension blunts venous return from the legs and gives false negatives.

90
00:06:27.601 --> 00:06:34.323
<v Tutor>Amputations, compression stockings and severe hypovolaemia with venoconstriction all reduce the volume you mobilise.

91
00:06:34.323 --> 00:06:37.738
<v Tutor>Pain, coughing and patient effort confound the reading.

92
00:06:37.738 --> 00:06:42.021
<v Tutor>And finally, a positive test tells you the heart will respond to fluid.

93
00:06:42.021 --> 00:06:44.840
<v Tutor>It doesn't tell you the patient needs fluid.

94
00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:46.455
<v Tutor>Now the twist.

95
00:06:46.455 --> 00:06:48.775
<v Tutor>Back to our mitral repair patient.

96
00:06:48.775 --> 00:06:52.972
<v Tutor>He's in atrial fibrillation, so pulse pressure variation is useless.

97
00:06:52.972 --> 00:06:55.900
<v Tutor>The rhythm varies stroke volume beat to beat.

98
00:06:55.900 --> 00:07:01.644
<v Tutor>The C V P of eighteen has a giant c v wave, which tells you there's new tricuspid regurgitation.

99
00:07:01.644 --> 00:07:10.922
<v Tutor>The echo shows a dilated right ventricle, a septum flattened in both systole and diastole, and a tricuspid annular plane systolic excursion of nine millimetres.

100
00:07:10.922 --> 00:07:15.120
<v Tutor>He's on a ventilator with a positive end-expiratory pressure of ten.

101
00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:17.260
<v Examiner>So, should he have the bolus?

102
00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:20.329
<v Candidate>I wouldn't give it on the C V P alone.

103
00:07:20.329 --> 00:07:28.539
<v Candidate>In atrial fibrillation with tricuspid regurgitation, the C V P mostly reflects the regurgitant v wave and a stiff, dilated right ventricle.

104
00:07:28.539 --> 00:07:32.728
<v Candidate>I'd do a passive leg raise with the velocity time integral on echo.

105
00:07:32.728 --> 00:07:36.526
<v Candidate>But with this right ventricle, I'd expect it to be negative.

106
00:07:36.526 --> 00:07:40.883
<v Candidate>A failing right ventricle sits on the flat part of its Starling curve.

107
00:07:40.883 --> 00:07:48.087
<v Candidate>Extra volume dilates it further, pushes the septum left, squashes the left ventricle and worsens tricuspid regurgitation.

108
00:07:48.087 --> 00:07:50.880
<v Candidate>That lowers cardiac output, not raises it.

109
00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:51.893
<v Examiner>Good.

110
00:07:51.893 --> 00:07:54.220
<v Examiner>What else tells you he's congested rather than dry?

111
00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:56.553
<v Candidate>Venous excess ultrasound.

112
00:07:56.553 --> 00:07:59.275
<v Candidate>Hepatic vein flow with systolic reversal.

113
00:07:59.275 --> 00:08:03.719
<v Candidate>A portal vein pulsatility fraction above fifty percent, which is severe.

114
00:08:03.719 --> 00:08:06.107
<v Candidate>Interrupted intrarenal venous flow.

115
00:08:06.107 --> 00:08:10.440
<v Candidate>Those suggest venous congestion that will harm the kidney and the gut.

116
00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:11.700
<v Examiner>And your management?

117
00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:13.278
<v Candidate>Avoid fluid.

118
00:08:13.278 --> 00:08:15.110
<v Candidate>Treat the right ventricle.

119
00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:22.115
<v Candidate>Keep the mean arterial pressure up with noradrenaline, and add vasopressin if needed, to protect right coronary perfusion.

120
00:08:22.115 --> 00:08:26.749
<v Candidate>Lower pulmonary vascular resistance with inhaled nitric oxide or prostacyclin.

121
00:08:26.749 --> 00:08:29.443
<v Candidate>Correct hypoxia, hypercapnia and acidosis.

122
00:08:29.443 --> 00:08:33.431
<v Candidate>Add an inotrope such as milrinone or dobutamine for contractility.

123
00:08:33.431 --> 00:08:39.681
<v Candidate>Rate or rhythm control for the atrial fibrillation, because the right ventricle tolerates tachycardia badly.

124
00:08:39.681 --> 00:08:45.393
<v Candidate>Reduce the positive end-expiratory pressure to the lowest level that keeps oxygenation acceptable.

125
00:08:45.393 --> 00:08:48.680
<v Candidate>If he's overloaded, use diuretics or ultrafiltration.

126
00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:50.779
<v Examiner>That's the answer I'm after.

127
00:08:50.779 --> 00:08:53.489
<v Examiner>The trap is treating a high C V P as a volume number.

128
00:08:53.489 --> 00:08:58.020
<v Examiner>It's a pressure, and in this patient it's telling you about the right ventricle and the valve.

129
00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:03.751
<v Tutor>So the spiral lesson is that the passive leg raise answers one question: will cardiac output rise?

130
00:09:03.751 --> 00:09:06.759
<v Tutor>Whether the patient should get fluid is a second question.

131
00:09:06.759 --> 00:09:10.040
<v Tutor>Answering it means looking at the right ventricle and the veins.

132
00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:14.504
<v Tutor>Imagine a letter arriving from the hospital executive.

133
00:09:14.504 --> 00:09:20.433
<v Tutor>Your unit's standardised mortality ratio is above the ninety-five percent confidence limit for comparable units.

134
00:09:20.433 --> 00:09:23.002
<v Tutor>The director wants an explanation by Friday.

135
00:09:23.002 --> 00:09:24.880
<v Tutor>What's the first thing you do?

136
00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:30.057
<v Tutor>This is the 2026 first sitting, question three, and it's in today's set.

137
00:09:30.057 --> 00:09:41.580
<v Tutor>Your ICU's Standardised Mortality Ratio, S M R, over the last 12 months has been noted to be above the 95 percent confidence interval, C I, for comparable ICUs in the National database.

138
00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:44.506
<v Tutor>Part a: Outline the limitations of S M R.

139
00:09:44.506 --> 00:09:45.700
<v Tutor>Three marks.

140
00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:49.761
<v Tutor>Part b: Outline your approach to investigating a high S M R.

141
00:09:49.761 --> 00:09:50.955
<v Tutor>Seven marks.

142
00:09:50.955 --> 00:09:53.761
<v Tutor>Just over half of candidates passed it.

143
00:09:53.761 --> 00:09:58.777
<v Tutor>The S M R has now been asked five times, in 2007 twice, 2015, 2019 and 2026.

144
00:09:58.777 --> 00:10:03.374
<v Tutor>In the 2015 second sitting version, only twenty-seven percent passed.

145
00:10:03.374 --> 00:10:09.345
<v Tutor>The examiners called it a significant knowledge gap about a commonly used quality indicator.

146
00:10:09.345 --> 00:10:14.480
<v Tutor>So this is a theme where knowing the structure lifts you well above the field.

147
00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:17.117
<v Tutor>First, the definition, in one line.

148
00:10:17.117 --> 00:10:21.382
<v Tutor>The S M R is observed hospital deaths divided by expected hospital deaths.

149
00:10:21.382 --> 00:10:26.947
<v Tutor>Expected deaths are the sum of each patient's predicted risk of death from a risk-adjustment model.

150
00:10:26.947 --> 00:10:33.605
<v Tutor>In Australia and New Zealand, the ANZICS Adult Patient Database uses the Australian and New Zealand Risk of Death model.

151
00:10:33.605 --> 00:10:37.662
<v Tutor>That model was built on APACHE three physiology, plus other variables.

152
00:10:37.662 --> 00:10:41.199
<v Tutor>An S M R of one means you had the deaths the model expected.

153
00:10:41.199 --> 00:10:43.800
<v Tutor>Above one means more deaths than expected.

154
00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:45.993
<v Tutor>Part a, the limitations.

155
00:10:45.993 --> 00:10:48.290
<v Tutor>Three marks, so about six points.

156
00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:51.651
<v Tutor>First, it's only as good as the model's calibration.

157
00:10:51.651 --> 00:10:54.677
<v Tutor>Mortality falls year on year, so models drift.

158
00:10:54.677 --> 00:10:58.262
<v Tutor>If the model isn't recalibrated, everyone's S M R falls.

159
00:10:58.262 --> 00:11:01.120
<v Tutor>Second, case mix the model doesn't capture.

160
00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:08.011
<v Tutor>Examples are a new ECMO service, a lot of patients with treatment limitations, or a referral unit taking transfers.

161
00:11:08.011 --> 00:11:09.692
<v Tutor>Third, lead-time bias.

162
00:11:09.692 --> 00:11:16.919
<v Tutor>Patients resuscitated in the emergency department or another hospital arrive with better physiology than their true risk.

163
00:11:16.919 --> 00:11:20.169
<v Tutor>Their predicted risk is lower, so the S M R rises.

164
00:11:20.169 --> 00:11:21.793
<v Tutor>Fourth, data quality.

165
00:11:21.793 --> 00:11:29.301
<v Tutor>The Glasgow Coma Scale in sedated patients, the admission diagnosis and the source of admission all move predicted risk a lot.

166
00:11:29.301 --> 00:11:32.662
<v Tutor>Fifth, the outcome is hospital death, not ICU death.

167
00:11:32.662 --> 00:11:38.265
<v Tutor>Deaths on the ward after discharge, and transfers out to die elsewhere, both move the ratio.

168
00:11:38.265 --> 00:11:40.114
<v Tutor>Sixth, statistical noise.

169
00:11:40.114 --> 00:11:42.971
<v Tutor>A small unit has wide confidence intervals.

170
00:11:42.971 --> 00:11:45.884
<v Tutor>One bad quarter can cross a limit by chance.

171
00:11:45.884 --> 00:11:48.630
<v Tutor>And seventh, it's a single crude outcome.

172
00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:53.840
<v Tutor>It says nothing about morbidity, function or patient experience, and it can be gamed.

173
00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:55.500
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

174
00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:59.960
<v Tutor>Name three reasons an S M R can rise when the quality of care hasn't changed at all.

175
00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:06.775
<v Tutor>Any three of these.

176
00:12:06.775 --> 00:12:10.757
<v Tutor>Poor data entry, especially Glasgow Coma Scale and diagnosis coding.

177
00:12:10.757 --> 00:12:14.058
<v Tutor>Lead-time bias from resuscitation before ICU admission.

178
00:12:14.058 --> 00:12:16.782
<v Tutor>A case-mix change the model doesn't capture.

179
00:12:16.782 --> 00:12:19.088
<v Tutor>A model that's no longer calibrated.

180
00:12:19.088 --> 00:12:23.541
<v Tutor>Changes in discharge practice, such as fewer patients transferred out to die.

181
00:12:23.541 --> 00:12:25.480
<v Tutor>And chance in a small sample.

182
00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:28.613
<v Tutor>Part b, seven marks, is about approach.

183
00:12:28.613 --> 00:12:33.031
<v Tutor>The examiners want a structure that reads like a real investigation.

184
00:12:33.031 --> 00:12:35.880
<v Tutor>Use four steps, and say them as headings.

185
00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:38.337
<v Tutor>Step one: is the signal real?

186
00:12:38.337 --> 00:12:39.950
<v Tutor>Check the statistics.

187
00:12:39.950 --> 00:12:42.786
<v Tutor>Where does the unit sit on the funnel plot?

188
00:12:42.786 --> 00:12:45.733
<v Tutor>Is it above the inner limit or the outer one?

189
00:12:45.733 --> 00:12:51.516
<v Tutor>Is it a single period, or a trend on a cumulative sum chart or a variable life-adjusted display?

190
00:12:51.516 --> 00:12:55.520
<v Tutor>Is the denominator large enough for the result to mean anything?

191
00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:58.091
<v Tutor>Step two: is the data right?

192
00:12:58.091 --> 00:13:01.708
<v Tutor>Audit the submissions to the Adult Patient Database.

193
00:13:01.708 --> 00:13:11.537
<v Tutor>Look at missing physiology, the worst Glasgow Coma Scale in sedated patients, diagnostic codes, the admission source, and elective versus emergency status.

194
00:13:11.537 --> 00:13:16.240
<v Tutor>A common College point is that miscoded data alone can move the S M R.

195
00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:19.238
<v Tutor>Step three: is it case mix or the model?

196
00:13:19.238 --> 00:13:25.082
<v Tutor>Break observed and expected deaths down by diagnostic group, by admission source and by risk decile.

197
00:13:25.082 --> 00:13:30.656
<v Tutor>If the excess sits in one group, such as cardiac arrests or ECMO, that tells you where to look.

198
00:13:30.656 --> 00:13:34.120
<v Tutor>Check whether the model is calibrated for your patients.

199
00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:36.380
<v Tutor>Step four: is it real care?

200
00:13:36.380 --> 00:13:38.877
<v Tutor>Look at structure, process and outcome.

201
00:13:38.877 --> 00:13:44.084
<v Tutor>Structure means staffing, senior cover after hours, occupancy, and after-hours discharges.

202
00:13:44.084 --> 00:13:52.320
<v Tutor>Process means delays to admission from the emergency department and wards, the rapid response system, adherence to protocols, and end-of-life care.

203
00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:57.527
<v Tutor>Outcome means structured mortality review of every death, with a preventability judgement.

204
00:13:57.527 --> 00:13:59.546
<v Tutor>And look beyond the ICU walls.

205
00:13:59.546 --> 00:14:01.724
<v Tutor>Has the surgical service changed?

206
00:14:01.724 --> 00:14:03.478
<v Tutor>Has a new service opened?

207
00:14:03.478 --> 00:14:06.400
<v Tutor>What's happening to deaths after ICU discharge?

208
00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:08.236
<v Tutor>Then close the loop.

209
00:14:08.236 --> 00:14:14.443
<v Tutor>Report to the hospital's clinical governance committee, make an action plan with owners and dates, and remeasure.

210
00:14:14.443 --> 00:14:18.701
<v Tutor>That's an audit cycle, and saying the phrase 'audit cycle' is worth a mark.

211
00:14:18.701 --> 00:14:24.600
<v Tutor>ANZICS CORE also has a formal process for following up outlier units, so mention that you'd engage with it.

212
00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:26.126
<v Tutor>Now the twist.

213
00:14:26.126 --> 00:14:28.327
<v Tutor>Your unit is half cardiac surgical.

214
00:14:28.327 --> 00:14:32.524
<v Tutor>Your S M R has risen from point seven to one point one over twelve months.

215
00:14:32.524 --> 00:14:35.543
<v Tutor>Here's a real-world check on why the College cares.

216
00:14:35.543 --> 00:14:44.398
<v Tutor>Solomon and colleagues analysed over five hundred and twenty thousand patients from one hundred and forty-four ICUs in the Adult Patient Database, from 2000 to 2010.

217
00:14:44.398 --> 00:14:48.338
<v Tutor>They used funnel plots with limits adjusted for multiple comparisons.

218
00:14:48.338 --> 00:14:55.197
<v Tutor>Seven ICUs stood out as performing unusually, all of them as poor performers, and four of the seven were in private hospitals.

219
00:14:55.197 --> 00:14:59.240
<v Tutor>So outlier status in this region is uncommon, and it's taken seriously.

220
00:15:00.140 --> 00:15:05.168
<v Examiner>Your cardiac surgical volume fell by a third this year because a second hospital opened a cardiac unit.

221
00:15:05.168 --> 00:15:06.300
<v Examiner>Does that matter?

222
00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:07.892
<v Candidate>Yes, a lot.

223
00:15:07.892 --> 00:15:13.870
<v Candidate>Elective cardiac surgical patients have low predicted mortality and very low observed mortality.

224
00:15:13.870 --> 00:15:17.147
<v Candidate>They pad the denominator and pull the S M R down.

225
00:15:17.147 --> 00:15:24.332
<v Candidate>If you lose a third of them, the remaining medical case mix dominates, and any miscalibration in that group shows up.

226
00:15:24.332 --> 00:15:31.747
<v Candidate>So I'd recalculate the S M R excluding elective cardiac surgery and compare it with the previous years on the same basis.

227
00:15:31.747 --> 00:15:36.920
<v Candidate>If the medical S M R hasn't changed, this is a case-mix shift, not a care problem.

228
00:15:37.420 --> 00:15:39.220
<v Examiner>And if the medical S M R has risen?

229
00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:41.735
<v Candidate>Then I'd move to step four.

230
00:15:41.735 --> 00:15:51.520
<v Candidate>I'd look at deaths by diagnosis, delays from the wards, after-hours discharges and readmissions within forty-eight hours, with a structured review of every death.

231
00:15:52.020 --> 00:15:52.546
<v Examiner>Good.

232
00:15:52.546 --> 00:15:55.948
<v Examiner>The trap in this question is jumping straight to blaming staff or protocols.

233
00:15:55.948 --> 00:15:58.620
<v Examiner>The College rewards checking the data and the model first.

234
00:16:00.020 --> 00:16:05.494
<v Tutor>Here's a woman whose bicarbonate is five, whose lactate is one, and whose ketones barely register.

235
00:16:05.494 --> 00:16:07.199
<v Tutor>Something is making acid.

236
00:16:07.199 --> 00:16:08.180
<v Tutor>What is it?

237
00:16:08.580 --> 00:16:12.732
<v Tutor>This is the 2014 second sitting, question twenty-three.

238
00:16:12.732 --> 00:16:25.322
<v Tutor>A 72-year-old female, body mass index 17.5 kilograms per metre squared, is admitted to the High Dependency Unit following a Medical Emergency Team call for tachypnoea and hypotension.

239
00:16:25.322 --> 00:16:37.515
<v Tutor>She is known to have sepsis relating to a urinary tract infection and wound infection following extensive surgery for resection of a left thigh chondrosarcoma six weeks earlier.

240
00:16:37.515 --> 00:16:39.691
<v Tutor>Here are the key results.

241
00:16:39.691 --> 00:16:44.107
<v Tutor>Arterial gas on an inspired oxygen fraction of point three.

242
00:16:44.107 --> 00:16:45.161
<v Tutor>pH 7.18.

243
00:16:45.161 --> 00:16:47.995
<v Tutor>P C O 2, 14 millimetres of mercury.

244
00:16:47.995 --> 00:16:50.896
<v Tutor>Bicarbonate, 5 millimoles per litre.

245
00:16:50.896 --> 00:16:52.873
<v Tutor>Base excess, minus 22.

246
00:16:52.873 --> 00:16:55.575
<v Tutor>Sodium, 141 millimoles per litre.

247
00:16:55.575 --> 00:16:57.025
<v Tutor>Chloride, 114.

248
00:16:57.025 --> 00:16:58.410
<v Tutor>Lactate, 1.0.

249
00:16:58.410 --> 00:16:59.794
<v Tutor>Glucose, 6.9.

250
00:16:59.794 --> 00:17:02.760
<v Tutor>Creatinine, 179 micromoles per litre.

251
00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:10.999
<v Tutor>Urinalysis shows 5-oxoproline, pyroglutamic acid, more than three plus, at 16 millimoles per millimole of creatinine.

252
00:17:10.999 --> 00:17:15.217
<v Tutor>Beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate are each one plus.

253
00:17:15.217 --> 00:17:18.974
<v Tutor>The parts are: a, interpret her acid-base status.

254
00:17:18.974 --> 00:17:23.983
<v Tutor>Then, with reference to her urinalysis: b, explain the significance.

255
00:17:23.983 --> 00:17:28.202
<v Tutor>c, list the likely predisposing factors in this patient.

256
00:17:28.202 --> 00:17:32.025
<v Tutor>d, briefly outline the underlying pathophysiology.

257
00:17:32.025 --> 00:17:34.859
<v Tutor>e, list your management strategies.

258
00:17:34.859 --> 00:17:37.100
<v Tutor>Fifty-five percent passed.

259
00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:39.453
<v Tutor>You've written this one twice.

260
00:17:39.453 --> 00:17:46.856
<v Tutor>You scored four and a half on the fourteenth of October, and two on the nineteenth, when you answered only part a and left b to e blank.

261
00:17:46.856 --> 00:17:50.403
<v Tutor>It's in today's set again, so this revisit is your rehearsal.

262
00:17:50.403 --> 00:17:56.160
<v Tutor>Pyroglutamic acidosis has appeared six times in the question bank, from 2011 to the 2025 second sitting.

263
00:17:56.160 --> 00:17:58.628
<v Tutor>It's usually one part of a gas question.

264
00:17:58.628 --> 00:18:02.740
<v Tutor>So the diagnosis has to come quickly, and the mechanism has to be ready.

265
00:18:03.140 --> 00:18:05.292
<v Tutor>Part a, with the numbers.

266
00:18:05.292 --> 00:18:07.705
<v Tutor>The College wants them shown.

267
00:18:07.705 --> 00:18:14.944
<v Tutor>Anion gap, using the College convention without potassium: 141 minus the sum of 114 and 5, which is 22.

268
00:18:14.944 --> 00:18:18.530
<v Tutor>Delta ratio: the gap rose by 10, from 12 to 22.

269
00:18:18.530 --> 00:18:21.465
<v Tutor>Bicarbonate fell by 19, from 24 to 5.

270
00:18:21.465 --> 00:18:24.465
<v Tutor>Ten over nineteen is about point five.

271
00:18:24.465 --> 00:18:31.768
<v Tutor>Expected P C O 2 by Winter's formula: one and a half times 5, plus 8, is 15 and a half, plus or minus 2.

272
00:18:31.768 --> 00:18:36.333
<v Tutor>Her P C O 2 is 14, so respiratory compensation is appropriate.

273
00:18:36.333 --> 00:18:43.115
<v Tutor>The apparent strong ion difference, sodium plus potassium minus chloride, is 31.3, which is low.

274
00:18:43.115 --> 00:18:52.180
<v Tutor>So the interpretation is a mixed high anion gap and normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, with appropriate respiratory compensation.

275
00:18:52.580 --> 00:18:53.378
<v Tutor>Part b.

276
00:18:53.378 --> 00:18:57.366
<v Tutor>The pyroglutamic acid is the unmeasured anion causing the high gap.

277
00:18:57.366 --> 00:19:00.450
<v Tutor>The lactate is normal and the ketones are trivial.

278
00:19:00.450 --> 00:19:03.906
<v Tutor>The ketones reflect starvation in a malnourished patient.

279
00:19:03.906 --> 00:19:05.980
<v Tutor>They don't explain a gap of 22.

280
00:19:06.380 --> 00:19:08.753
<v Tutor>Part c, the predisposing factors.

281
00:19:08.753 --> 00:19:09.622
<v Tutor>Female.

282
00:19:09.622 --> 00:19:10.548
<v Tutor>Elderly.

283
00:19:10.548 --> 00:19:13.616
<v Tutor>Malnourished, with a body mass index of 17.5.

284
00:19:13.616 --> 00:19:14.484
<v Tutor>Sepsis.

285
00:19:14.484 --> 00:19:17.379
<v Tutor>Renal impairment, which reduces excretion.

286
00:19:17.379 --> 00:19:19.405
<v Tutor>Likely regular paracetamol.

287
00:19:19.405 --> 00:19:25.830
<v Tutor>Likely flucloxacillin for a post-operative wound infection, since staphylococcus is the usual organism.

288
00:19:25.830 --> 00:19:28.030
<v Tutor>Liver dysfunction, if present.

289
00:19:28.030 --> 00:19:32.140
<v Tutor>A congenital enzyme deficiency is very unlikely at seventy-two.

290
00:19:32.540 --> 00:19:33.970
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

291
00:19:33.970 --> 00:19:38.260
<v Tutor>Part d: what are the two mechanisms that make 5-oxoproline accumulate?

292
00:19:43.660 --> 00:19:45.703
<v Tutor>First, glutathione depletion.

293
00:19:45.703 --> 00:19:51.834
<v Tutor>Glutathione normally inhibits the first enzyme of its own synthesis, gamma-glutamylcysteine synthetase.

294
00:19:51.834 --> 00:19:55.589
<v Tutor>Sepsis, malnutrition and paracetamol all use up glutathione.

295
00:19:55.589 --> 00:20:00.450
<v Tutor>Paracetamol does it through its toxic metabolite, N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine.

296
00:20:00.450 --> 00:20:03.377
<v Tutor>When glutathione falls, that brake comes off.

297
00:20:03.377 --> 00:20:09.728
<v Tutor>The cycle runs fast, and gamma-glutamylcysteine is converted to 5-oxoproline faster than it can be cleared.

298
00:20:09.728 --> 00:20:15.914
<v Tutor>Second, flucloxacillin inhibits 5-oxoprolinase, the enzyme that converts 5-oxoproline back to glutamate.

299
00:20:15.914 --> 00:20:19.724
<v Tutor>So one drug floods the cycle, and the other blocks the drain.

300
00:20:19.724 --> 00:20:24.143
<v Tutor>In October you wrote that beta-lactams are metabolised to pyroglutamate.

301
00:20:24.143 --> 00:20:25.247
<v Tutor>They aren't.

302
00:20:25.247 --> 00:20:28.340
<v Tutor>Flucloxacillin blocks the enzyme that clears it.

303
00:20:28.740 --> 00:20:30.210
<v Tutor>Part e, management.

304
00:20:30.210 --> 00:20:31.789
<v Tutor>Stop the paracetamol.

305
00:20:31.789 --> 00:20:35.382
<v Tutor>Change flucloxacillin to an alternative such as cefazolin.

306
00:20:35.382 --> 00:20:38.703
<v Tutor>Treat the sepsis and get source control of the wound.

307
00:20:38.703 --> 00:20:45.235
<v Tutor>Give N-acetylcysteine to replenish glutathione, usually using the intravenous regimen for paracetamol poisoning.

308
00:20:45.235 --> 00:20:47.685
<v Tutor>Improve nutrition, and give thiamine.

309
00:20:47.685 --> 00:20:50.788
<v Tutor>Then supportive care, with monitoring of the gas.

310
00:20:50.788 --> 00:20:55.470
<v Tutor>Bicarbonate and dialysis are only for extreme acidaemia or another indication.

311
00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:57.539
<v Tutor>In October you put them first.

312
00:20:57.539 --> 00:20:58.900
<v Tutor>They belong last.

313
00:20:59.300 --> 00:21:06.068
<v Tutor>Now the twist, and it ties to this week's cardiac theme and to today's journal watch.

314
00:21:06.068 --> 00:21:09.634
<v Tutor>Day eight after coronary bypass grafting.

315
00:21:09.634 --> 00:21:14.582
<v Tutor>A sixty-eight-year-old woman weighing forty-eight kilograms.

316
00:21:14.582 --> 00:21:21.059
<v Tutor>She's on flucloxacillin for a sternal wound infection and on regular paracetamol.

317
00:21:21.059 --> 00:21:25.062
<v Tutor>And her empagliflozin was restarted on day two.

318
00:21:25.062 --> 00:21:26.881
<v Tutor>Her gas: pH 7.20.

319
00:21:26.881 --> 00:21:28.337
<v Tutor>P C O 2, 20.

320
00:21:28.337 --> 00:21:30.010
<v Tutor>Bicarbonate, 8.

321
00:21:30.010 --> 00:21:31.466
<v Tutor>Sodium, 138.

322
00:21:31.466 --> 00:21:33.067
<v Tutor>Chloride, 108.

323
00:21:33.067 --> 00:21:34.595
<v Tutor>Lactate, 1.2.

324
00:21:34.595 --> 00:21:35.978
<v Tutor>Glucose, 9.

325
00:21:35.978 --> 00:21:40.999
<v Tutor>A capillary beta-hydroxybutyrate of 4.8 millimoles per litre.

326
00:21:40.999 --> 00:21:45.220
<v Tutor>The urine dipstick shows ketones at only one plus.

327
00:21:46.120 --> 00:21:46.960
<v Examiner>What's going on?

328
00:21:47.460 --> 00:21:52.313
<v Candidate>The anion gap is 138 minus the sum of 108 and 8, which is 22.

329
00:21:52.313 --> 00:21:59.627
<v Candidate>Expected P C O 2 is one and a half times 8, plus 8, which is 20, so compensation is appropriate.

330
00:21:59.627 --> 00:22:04.409
<v Candidate>There's a euglycaemic ketoacidosis from the SGLT2 inhibitor.

331
00:22:04.409 --> 00:22:08.980
<v Candidate>The beta-hydroxybutyrate is 4.8 with a glucose of only 9.

332
00:22:09.480 --> 00:22:11.320
<v Examiner>Does the ketone explain the whole gap?

333
00:22:11.820 --> 00:22:12.446
<v Candidate>No.

334
00:22:12.446 --> 00:22:14.265
<v Candidate>The gap has risen by 10.

335
00:22:14.265 --> 00:22:19.838
<v Candidate>The beta-hydroxybutyrate accounts for about 5 of that, and the lactate for almost nothing.

336
00:22:19.838 --> 00:22:23.989
<v Candidate>So about 5 millimoles per litre of unmeasured anion is left over.

337
00:22:23.989 --> 00:22:30.756
<v Candidate>With flucloxacillin, paracetamol, malnutrition and sepsis, that's pyroglutamic acidosis until proven otherwise.

338
00:22:30.756 --> 00:22:32.860
<v Candidate>I'd send urine organic acids.

339
00:22:33.360 --> 00:22:35.280
<v Examiner>Why is the dipstick only one plus?

340
00:22:35.780 --> 00:22:40.720
<v Candidate>The dipstick's nitroprusside reaction detects acetoacetate, not beta-hydroxybutyrate.

341
00:22:40.720 --> 00:22:44.333
<v Candidate>Beta-hydroxybutyrate is the dominant ketone in ketoacidosis.

342
00:22:44.333 --> 00:22:46.936
<v Candidate>So a dipstick can badly underestimate it.

343
00:22:46.936 --> 00:22:49.220
<v Candidate>I'd use blood beta-hydroxybutyrate.

344
00:22:49.720 --> 00:22:50.440
<v Examiner>Management?

345
00:22:50.940 --> 00:22:54.486
<v Candidate>Stop the empagliflozin, paracetamol and flucloxacillin.

346
00:22:54.486 --> 00:22:57.413
<v Candidate>Use an alternative antistaphylococcal agent.

347
00:22:57.413 --> 00:23:00.790
<v Candidate>Start an insulin infusion with intravenous dextrose.

348
00:23:00.790 --> 00:23:07.882
<v Candidate>The ketoacidosis needs insulin even though the glucose is normal, and the dextrose lets me give enough insulin safely.

349
00:23:07.882 --> 00:23:10.809
<v Candidate>Give N-acetylcysteine for the pyroglutamate.

350
00:23:10.809 --> 00:23:12.948
<v Candidate>Watch potassium and phosphate.

351
00:23:12.948 --> 00:23:16.100
<v Candidate>And start nutrition, which treats both problems.

352
00:23:16.600 --> 00:23:17.152
<v Examiner>Good.

353
00:23:17.152 --> 00:23:19.147
<v Examiner>The examiner's point is the arithmetic.

354
00:23:19.147 --> 00:23:24.200
<v Examiner>If you calculate the gap and subtract the anions you can measure, the leftover tells you there's a second acid.

355
00:23:25.600 --> 00:23:27.388
<v Tutor>Newcastle upon Tyne, 1891.

356
00:23:27.388 --> 00:23:34.120
<v Tutor>A doctor three years out of medical school is about to inject a woman with an extract made from a sheep's thyroid gland.

357
00:23:34.520 --> 00:23:36.885
<v Tutor>His name was George Redmayne Murray.

358
00:23:36.885 --> 00:23:39.842
<v Tutor>Myxoedema was a well-described disease by then.

359
00:23:39.842 --> 00:23:42.314
<v Tutor>William Gull had described it in 1873.

360
00:23:42.314 --> 00:23:46.776
<v Tutor>A Clinical Society committee chaired by William Ord reported on it in 1888.

361
00:23:46.776 --> 00:23:49.248
<v Tutor>They linked it to loss of the thyroid.

362
00:23:49.248 --> 00:23:51.130
<v Tutor>But there was no treatment.

363
00:23:51.130 --> 00:23:54.892
<v Tutor>Patients became slow, cold, puffy and confused, and many died.

364
00:23:54.892 --> 00:24:01.934
<v Tutor>In 1890 two doctors in Lisbon, Bettencourt and Serrano, had tried implanting half a sheep's thyroid under a patient's skin.

365
00:24:01.934 --> 00:24:07.793
<v Tutor>Murray reasoned that if the gland made something the body needed, an extract might work just as well.

366
00:24:07.793 --> 00:24:11.233
<v Tutor>He prepared a glycerin extract of healthy sheep thyroid.

367
00:24:11.233 --> 00:24:17.845
<v Tutor>From April 1891, he injected one and a half millilitres under the skin of a forty-six-year-old woman, twice a week.

368
00:24:17.845 --> 00:24:20.425
<v Tutor>Within three months she was transformed.

369
00:24:20.425 --> 00:24:23.919
<v Tutor>He published it in the British Medical Journal that year.

370
00:24:23.919 --> 00:24:27.520
<v Tutor>And his patient went on to live until she was seventy-four.

371
00:24:27.920 --> 00:24:32.065
<v Tutor>It was one of the first times anyone had replaced a missing hormone.

372
00:24:32.065 --> 00:24:35.938
<v Tutor>Within a year or two others showed the extract worked by mouth.

373
00:24:35.938 --> 00:24:39.375
<v Tutor>Thyroxine was isolated in 1914 and synthesised in 1927.

374
00:24:39.375 --> 00:24:42.429
<v Tutor>Murray's reasoning still shapes the exam answer.

375
00:24:42.429 --> 00:24:45.920
<v Tutor>The hormone works, but the old heart can't take it fast.

376
00:24:45.920 --> 00:24:50.720
<v Tutor>Replacement in the elderly and in coronary disease is still about dose and pace.

377
00:24:51.120 --> 00:24:55.070
<v Tutor>This is the 2014 first sitting, question thirty, part one.

378
00:24:55.070 --> 00:25:03.030
<v Tutor>You are asked to review an 80-year-old female in the Emergency Department who has presented with a depressed conscious state.

379
00:25:03.030 --> 00:25:07.519
<v Tutor>She has ischaemic heart disease and paroxysmal atrial fibrillation.

380
00:25:07.519 --> 00:25:11.588
<v Tutor>Her medication includes aspirin, metoprolol, and amiodarone.

381
00:25:11.588 --> 00:25:22.960
<v Tutor>On examination she has a temperature of 34.5 degrees Celsius, she is drowsy with a G C S of 10, a pulse of 50 beats per minute and a blood pressure 90 over 40 millimetres of mercury.

382
00:25:22.960 --> 00:25:25.892
<v Tutor>C T brain scan shows age related atrophy.

383
00:25:25.892 --> 00:25:29.064
<v Tutor>Her results: sodium 120 millimoles per litre.

384
00:25:29.064 --> 00:25:30.381
<v Tutor>Potassium 4.0.

385
00:25:30.381 --> 00:25:31.399
<v Tutor>Urea 6.0.

386
00:25:31.399 --> 00:25:33.972
<v Tutor>Creatinine 90 micromoles per litre.

387
00:25:33.972 --> 00:25:35.887
<v Tutor>Measured osmolality 255.

388
00:25:35.887 --> 00:25:38.341
<v Tutor>Glucose 3.0 millimoles per litre.

389
00:25:38.341 --> 00:25:40.316
<v Tutor>C K 1000 units per litre.

390
00:25:40.316 --> 00:25:43.009
<v Tutor>Cholesterol 7.2 millimoles per litre.

391
00:25:43.009 --> 00:25:49.353
<v Tutor>Part a: Give the likely diagnosis and the underlying cause to account for all these blood results.

392
00:25:49.353 --> 00:25:54.680
<v Tutor>Part b: List four measures essential for the specific management of this patient.

393
00:25:55.080 --> 00:25:58.155
<v Tutor>You scored six on the thirteenth of October.

394
00:25:58.155 --> 00:26:00.047
<v Tutor>The diagnosis was right.

395
00:26:00.047 --> 00:26:05.428
<v Tutor>You missed the hydrocortisone, the thyroxine dosing and the sodium correction rate.

396
00:26:05.428 --> 00:26:10.809
<v Tutor>Myxoedema coma has come up at least five times, in 2008, 2014, 2018 twice and 2024.

397
00:26:10.809 --> 00:26:14.120
<v Tutor>So it's a reliable theme in the endocrine block.

398
00:26:14.520 --> 00:26:15.386
<v Tutor>Part a.

399
00:26:15.386 --> 00:26:20.235
<v Tutor>Myxoedema coma, which is decompensated hypothyroidism, caused by amiodarone.

400
00:26:20.235 --> 00:26:21.736
<v Tutor>Every result fits.

401
00:26:21.736 --> 00:26:22.890
<v Tutor>Hypothermia.

402
00:26:22.890 --> 00:26:24.045
<v Tutor>Bradycardia.

403
00:26:24.045 --> 00:26:27.277
<v Tutor>Hyponatraemia from reduced free water clearance.

404
00:26:27.277 --> 00:26:28.547
<v Tutor>Hypoglycaemia.

405
00:26:28.547 --> 00:26:31.260
<v Tutor>A raised creatine kinase from myopathy.

406
00:26:31.260 --> 00:26:33.223
<v Tutor>And hypercholesterolaemia.

407
00:26:33.223 --> 00:26:34.551
<v Tutor>Why amiodarone?

408
00:26:34.551 --> 00:26:37.841
<v Tutor>It's about thirty-seven percent iodine by weight.

409
00:26:37.841 --> 00:26:43.902
<v Tutor>The iodine load triggers the Wolff-Chaikoff effect, which switches off thyroid hormone synthesis.

410
00:26:43.902 --> 00:26:49.617
<v Tutor>Most people escape from it, but someone with underlying autoimmune thyroid disease may not.

411
00:26:49.617 --> 00:26:53.600
<v Tutor>Amiodarone also blocks the peripheral conversion of T4 to T3.

412
00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:55.462
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

413
00:26:55.462 --> 00:26:59.400
<v Tutor>Which drug goes in first, before the thyroid hormone, and why?

414
00:27:04.800 --> 00:27:10.385
<v Tutor>Hydrocortisone, for example 100 milligrams intravenously, then 50 milligrams six-hourly.

415
00:27:10.385 --> 00:27:17.482
<v Tutor>Hypothyroidism can coexist with adrenal insufficiency, from autoimmune polyglandular disease or a pituitary cause.

416
00:27:17.482 --> 00:27:23.008
<v Tutor>Thyroid hormone speeds up cortisol clearance and can tip a patient into adrenal crisis.

417
00:27:23.008 --> 00:27:27.080
<v Tutor>So you give the steroid first, after taking a random cortisol.

418
00:27:27.480 --> 00:27:29.548
<v Tutor>The other specific measures.

419
00:27:29.548 --> 00:27:30.926
<v Tutor>Thyroid hormone.

420
00:27:30.926 --> 00:27:41.897
<v Tutor>The College answer for this eighty-year-old with ischaemic heart disease accepted low-dose thyroxine, about 50 to 100 micrograms a day, increased slowly, with T3 as a cautious option.

421
00:27:41.897 --> 00:27:51.547
<v Tutor>Contemporary guidance, including the 2014 American Thyroid Association guideline, favours an intravenous loading dose of 200 to 400 micrograms of levothyroxine.

422
00:27:51.547 --> 00:27:56.371
<v Tutor>Use the lower end in the elderly, the small and those with coronary disease.

423
00:27:56.371 --> 00:27:59.128
<v Tutor>Then give daily intravenous maintenance.

424
00:27:59.128 --> 00:28:06.251
<v Tutor>Liothyronine, or T3, is optional in small doses, 5 to 20 micrograms to load, then 2.5 to 10 micrograms eight-hourly.

425
00:28:06.251 --> 00:28:09.812
<v Tutor>Be careful with it in coronary disease and arrhythmia.

426
00:28:09.812 --> 00:28:12.914
<v Tutor>Correct the glucose with intravenous dextrose.

427
00:28:12.914 --> 00:28:14.867
<v Tutor>Correct the sodium slowly.

428
00:28:14.867 --> 00:28:21.357
<v Tutor>The College answer used hypertonic saline aiming for 130, no faster than 2 millimoles per litre per hour.

429
00:28:21.357 --> 00:28:23.884
<v Tutor>Today you'd also cap the total rise.

430
00:28:23.884 --> 00:28:28.996
<v Tutor>Aim for no more than 8 to 10 millimoles per litre in the first twenty-four hours.

431
00:28:28.996 --> 00:28:34.568
<v Tutor>Elderly, malnourished and hypothyroid patients are at high risk of osmotic demyelination.

432
00:28:34.568 --> 00:28:36.521
<v Tutor>And use passive rewarming.

433
00:28:36.521 --> 00:28:41.920
<v Tutor>Active external rewarming can vasodilate a patient with no reserve and cause collapse.

434
00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:45.163
<v Tutor>Now the twist, which is pure cardiac week.

435
00:28:45.163 --> 00:28:47.721
<v Tutor>She's intubated for a rising P C O 2.

436
00:28:47.721 --> 00:28:50.962
<v Tutor>After induction her blood pressure is 75 over 35.

437
00:28:50.962 --> 00:28:53.975
<v Tutor>Her heart rate is 38, in a junctional rhythm.

438
00:28:53.975 --> 00:29:00.400
<v Tutor>Echo shows a moderate circumferential pericardial effusion, with no right atrial or ventricular collapse.

439
00:29:01.300 --> 00:29:03.860
<v Examiner>What's driving the bradycardia, and what will you do?

440
00:29:04.360 --> 00:29:06.199
<v Candidate>Several things together.

441
00:29:06.199 --> 00:29:10.223
<v Candidate>Hypothyroidism itself, hypothermia, metoprolol and amiodarone.

442
00:29:10.223 --> 00:29:11.832
<v Candidate>I'd stop both drugs.

443
00:29:11.832 --> 00:29:15.396
<v Candidate>I'd give hydrocortisone and start thyroid replacement.

444
00:29:15.396 --> 00:29:22.696
<v Candidate>Atropine may not work, so I'd use an adrenaline or isoprenaline infusion as a bridge, and set up transcutaneous pacing.

445
00:29:22.696 --> 00:29:27.697
<v Candidate>If she stays haemodynamically compromised, I'd place a transvenous pacing wire.

446
00:29:27.697 --> 00:29:32.640
<v Candidate>Amiodarone has a very long half-life, so its effects won't wear off for weeks.

447
00:29:33.140 --> 00:29:34.101
<v Examiner>And the effusion?

448
00:29:34.101 --> 00:29:35.100
<v Examiner>Will you drain it?

449
00:29:35.600 --> 00:29:38.231
<v Candidate>Not unless there's tamponade physiology.

450
00:29:38.231 --> 00:29:43.987
<v Candidate>Hypothyroid effusions accumulate slowly, so the pericardium stretches, and tamponade is uncommon.

451
00:29:43.987 --> 00:29:46.399
<v Candidate>This echo shows no chamber collapse.

452
00:29:46.399 --> 00:29:50.237
<v Candidate>The effusion will resolve as the thyroid hormone takes effect.

453
00:29:50.237 --> 00:29:53.800
<v Candidate>I'd re-image it, and drain it only if tamponade develops.

454
00:29:54.300 --> 00:29:56.740
<v Examiner>Why might she be refractory to vasopressors?

455
00:29:57.240 --> 00:30:03.164
<v Candidate>Untreated hypothyroidism reduces adrenergic receptor responsiveness and cardiac contractility.

456
00:30:03.164 --> 00:30:06.533
<v Candidate>Unrecognised adrenal insufficiency makes it worse.

457
00:30:06.533 --> 00:30:09.960
<v Candidate>That's another reason to give hydrocortisone early.

458
00:30:10.460 --> 00:30:11.048
<v Examiner>Good.

459
00:30:11.048 --> 00:30:13.173
<v Examiner>One more twist for the endocrine block.

460
00:30:13.173 --> 00:30:15.660
<v Examiner>Amiodarone can also cause the opposite problem.

461
00:30:16.560 --> 00:30:17.211
<v Tutor>Yes.

462
00:30:17.211 --> 00:30:20.520
<v Tutor>Amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis comes in two types.

463
00:30:20.520 --> 00:30:28.495
<v Tutor>Type one is iodine-induced excess hormone synthesis in an abnormal gland, such as a multinodular goitre, and it's treated with thionamides.

464
00:30:28.495 --> 00:30:32.944
<v Tutor>Type two is a destructive thyroiditis, and it's treated with prednisolone.

465
00:30:32.944 --> 00:30:34.409
<v Tutor>Mixed forms happen.

466
00:30:34.409 --> 00:30:41.082
<v Tutor>And a critically ill patient on amiodarone with low thyroid hormone levels may simply have sick euthyroid syndrome.

467
00:30:41.082 --> 00:30:46.995
<v Tutor>That's why the 2021 second sitting asked about hypothyroidism in the ICU and sick euthyroid together.

468
00:30:46.995 --> 00:30:48.731
<v Tutor>Don't treat the numbers.

469
00:30:48.731 --> 00:30:52.800
<v Tutor>Treat the patient who is cold, slow, hyponatraemic and bradycardic.

470
00:30:54.200 --> 00:30:58.862
<v Tutor>Two new trials this week, both summarised by The Bottom Line, and both about the kidney.

471
00:30:58.862 --> 00:31:01.435
<v Tutor>One asks how to dialyse the sickest patients.

472
00:31:01.435 --> 00:31:06.000
<v Tutor>The other asks whether a diabetes drug can protect the kidney through cardiac surgery.

473
00:31:06.400 --> 00:31:12.264
<v Tutor>The first is Gaudry and colleagues, published in the New England Journal of Medicine in October 2026.

474
00:31:12.264 --> 00:31:18.290
<v Tutor>It was an investigator-initiated, open-label, randomised non-inferiority trial in forty-two French ICUs.

475
00:31:18.290 --> 00:31:26.791
<v Tutor>It enrolled adults with acute kidney injury and an indication for renal replacement therapy who were on invasive ventilation, catecholamines, or both.

476
00:31:26.791 --> 00:31:32.924
<v Tutor>Patients were randomised to start with intermittent haemodialysis or continuous renal replacement therapy.

477
00:31:32.924 --> 00:31:36.905
<v Tutor>The primary outcome was major adverse kidney events at day ninety.

478
00:31:36.905 --> 00:31:40.779
<v Tutor>That means death, new dialysis, or persistent renal dysfunction.

479
00:31:40.779 --> 00:31:44.760
<v Tutor>The non-inferiority margin was seven and a half percentage points.

480
00:31:45.160 --> 00:31:50.933
<v Tutor>The trial planned for a thousand patients but stopped early at five hundred and fifty-four.

481
00:31:50.933 --> 00:31:58.747
<v Tutor>Major adverse kidney events occurred in 78.1 percent with intermittent dialysis, against 71.6 percent with continuous therapy.

482
00:31:58.747 --> 00:32:05.394
<v Tutor>The adjusted difference was 6.3 percentage points, with a confidence interval from minus 1.2 to plus 13.8.

483
00:32:05.394 --> 00:32:09.826
<v Tutor>The upper bound crosses the margin, so non-inferiority wasn't shown.

484
00:32:09.826 --> 00:32:18.631
<v Tutor>As a secondary outcome, mortality was higher with intermittent dialysis, with a hazard ratio of 1.30 and a confidence interval of 1.04 to 1.63.

485
00:32:18.631 --> 00:32:24.229
<v Tutor>Dialysis dependence at day ninety was low and similar in both groups, about two percent.

486
00:32:24.229 --> 00:32:29.360
<v Tutor>Twenty-two percent of the intermittent group crossed over to continuous therapy.

487
00:32:29.760 --> 00:32:31.413
<v Tutor>The limitations matter.

488
00:32:31.413 --> 00:32:38.822
<v Tutor>The trial stopped early, so it was underpowered, and a mortality signal from a secondary outcome should be treated as a hypothesis.

489
00:32:38.822 --> 00:32:44.047
<v Tutor>The delivered intermittent dose was on the low side, with a median Kt over V of about one.

490
00:32:44.047 --> 00:32:49.111
<v Tutor>It tested only the initial modality, it was open label, and it was done in one country.

491
00:32:49.111 --> 00:32:57.640
<v Tutor>Earlier trials, including the French Hemodiafe trial of about three hundred and sixty patients in 2006, found no survival difference between modalities.

492
00:32:58.040 --> 00:32:59.947
<v Tutor>Three take-home messages.

493
00:32:59.947 --> 00:33:09.945
<v Tutor>One: in ventilated or vasopressor-dependent patients with acute kidney injury, starting with intermittent dialysis was not shown to be as good as continuous therapy.

494
00:33:09.945 --> 00:33:17.631
<v Tutor>Two: there's a mortality signal favouring continuous therapy, but it comes from a secondary outcome in a trial stopped early.

495
00:33:17.631 --> 00:33:27.975
<v Tutor>Three: it supports what most Australian and New Zealand units already do, and what KDIGO 2012 suggests, which is continuous therapy for haemodynamically unstable patients.

496
00:33:27.975 --> 00:33:36.355
<v Tutor>For the exam, if you're asked to compare modalities, you can now name a 2026 trial that backs continuous therapy in the unstable patient.

497
00:33:36.355 --> 00:33:38.840
<v Tutor>Summary drawn from The Bottom Line.

498
00:33:39.240 --> 00:33:44.870
<v Tutor>The second study is MERCURI-2, by Oosterom-Eijmael and colleagues, in JAMA in 2026.

499
00:33:44.870 --> 00:33:50.562
<v Tutor>It was a double-blind, placebo-controlled randomised trial in seven Dutch hospitals.

500
00:33:50.562 --> 00:33:56.068
<v Tutor>It enrolled seven hundred and eighty-four adults having elective cardiac surgery.

501
00:33:56.068 --> 00:34:06.276
<v Tutor>They received dapagliflozin 10 milligrams or placebo, in four doses: the evening before surgery, the morning of surgery, and post-operative days one and two.

502
00:34:06.276 --> 00:34:11.720
<v Tutor>The primary outcome was acute kidney injury by KDIGO criteria within seven days.

503
00:34:12.120 --> 00:34:18.265
<v Tutor>Acute kidney injury occurred in 28 percent with dapagliflozin, against 52 percent with placebo.

504
00:34:18.265 --> 00:34:22.978
<v Tutor>The relative risk was 0.54, with a confidence interval of 0.45 to 0.65.

505
00:34:22.978 --> 00:34:25.245
<v Tutor>That looks like a huge effect.

506
00:34:25.245 --> 00:34:27.094
<v Tutor>But look underneath it.

507
00:34:27.094 --> 00:34:31.389
<v Tutor>The effect came almost entirely from the urine output criterion.

508
00:34:31.389 --> 00:34:38.906
<v Tutor>Defined by creatinine alone, acute kidney injury occurred in 14 percent versus 15 percent, so there was no difference.

509
00:34:38.906 --> 00:34:42.724
<v Tutor>Stage three injury was under one percent in both groups.

510
00:34:42.724 --> 00:34:46.960
<v Tutor>There was one case of ketoacidosis, in the dapagliflozin group.

511
00:34:47.360 --> 00:34:49.965
<v Tutor>Here's the physiology that explains it.

512
00:34:49.965 --> 00:34:54.731
<v Tutor>SGLT2 inhibitors block glucose and sodium reabsorption in the proximal tubule.

513
00:34:54.731 --> 00:34:57.835
<v Tutor>That causes an osmotic diuresis and natriuresis.

514
00:34:57.835 --> 00:35:00.551
<v Tutor>So the drug raises urine output directly.

515
00:35:00.551 --> 00:35:07.201
<v Tutor>That makes patients less likely to meet the oliguria criterion, whether or not the kidney is actually protected.

516
00:35:07.201 --> 00:35:09.640
<v Tutor>The outcome definition did the work.

517
00:35:10.040 --> 00:35:11.872
<v Tutor>Three take-home messages.

518
00:35:11.872 --> 00:35:18.647
<v Tutor>One: dapagliflozin halved KDIGO acute kidney injury after cardiac surgery, but only by the urine output criterion.

519
00:35:18.647 --> 00:35:23.422
<v Tutor>Two: creatinine-defined injury, severe injury and hard outcomes didn't change.

520
00:35:23.422 --> 00:35:25.754
<v Tutor>Three: it doesn't change practice.

521
00:35:25.754 --> 00:35:34.027
<v Tutor>Remember that perioperative SGLT2 inhibitors carry a risk of euglycaemic ketoacidosis, which is why they're normally withheld before surgery.

522
00:35:34.027 --> 00:35:41.412
<v Tutor>For the exam, this is a perfect example of how a composite or surrogate outcome can be driven by the drug's own pharmacology.

523
00:35:41.412 --> 00:35:43.800
<v Tutor>Summary drawn from The Bottom Line.

524
00:35:45.200 --> 00:35:47.115
<v Tutor>Now the syllabus round-up.

525
00:35:47.115 --> 00:35:50.270
<v Tutor>Six items, all beyond this week's cardiac block.

526
00:35:50.270 --> 00:35:53.200
<v Tutor>We'll go fast and focus on what earns marks.

527
00:35:53.600 --> 00:35:55.389
<v Tutor>First, renal malignancy.

528
00:35:55.389 --> 00:35:59.973
<v Tutor>It's rarely examined on its own, but it turns up in ICU as a complication.

529
00:35:59.973 --> 00:36:03.383
<v Tutor>Renal cell carcinoma causes paraneoplastic syndromes.

530
00:36:03.383 --> 00:36:07.296
<v Tutor>Hypercalcaemia comes from parathyroid hormone-related peptide.

531
00:36:07.296 --> 00:36:10.035
<v Tutor>Erythrocytosis comes from erythropoietin.

532
00:36:10.035 --> 00:36:12.159
<v Tutor>Hypertension comes from renin.

533
00:36:12.159 --> 00:36:16.743
<v Tutor>And there's a non-metastatic hepatic dysfunction called Stauffer syndrome.

534
00:36:16.743 --> 00:36:21.439
<v Tutor>The surgical problem is tumour thrombus extending up the inferior vena cava.

535
00:36:21.439 --> 00:36:26.023
<v Tutor>When it reaches the right atrium, removal may need cardiopulmonary bypass.

536
00:36:26.023 --> 00:36:33.793
<v Tutor>The ICU then deals with major haemorrhage, tumour embolism to the lungs, and acute kidney injury in a patient left with one kidney.

537
00:36:33.793 --> 00:36:36.141
<v Tutor>The modern twist is immunotherapy.

538
00:36:36.141 --> 00:36:43.856
<v Tutor>Patients on checkpoint inhibitors such as nivolumab and ipilimumab can arrive with myocarditis, pneumonitis, colitis or nephritis.

539
00:36:43.856 --> 00:36:48.551
<v Tutor>These are treated with high-dose corticosteroids, after excluding infection.

540
00:36:48.551 --> 00:36:52.800
<v Tutor>Myocarditis is the one that kills, so check troponin and do an echo.

541
00:36:53.200 --> 00:36:54.901
<v Tutor>How might it be asked?

542
00:36:54.901 --> 00:36:57.225
<v Tutor>Usually as a post-operative case.

543
00:36:57.225 --> 00:37:06.692
<v Tutor>For example: day one after radical nephrectomy with removal of a caval tumour thrombus, now hypotensive with a falling haemoglobin and rising airway pressures.

544
00:37:06.692 --> 00:37:12.077
<v Tutor>The structure that earns marks is a differential, then investigations, then management.

545
00:37:12.077 --> 00:37:14.345
<v Tutor>Bleeding is the commonest cause.

546
00:37:14.345 --> 00:37:19.787
<v Tutor>Tumour or thrombus embolism shows up as sudden right ventricular failure and hypoxaemia.

547
00:37:19.787 --> 00:37:23.472
<v Tutor>Abdominal compartment syndrome follows a big transfusion.

548
00:37:23.472 --> 00:37:26.816
<v Tutor>And there's acute kidney injury in a single kidney.

549
00:37:26.816 --> 00:37:29.424
<v Tutor>Treat the solitary kidney as precious.

550
00:37:29.424 --> 00:37:36.000
<v Tutor>Avoid nephrotoxins, dose drugs for the new glomerular filtration rate, and keep perfusion pressure adequate.

551
00:37:36.400 --> 00:37:44.229
<v Tutor>Second, respiratory monitoring, which has appeared in about two dozen past questions and leads into next week's respiratory block.

552
00:37:44.229 --> 00:37:45.874
<v Tutor>Think in four layers.

553
00:37:45.874 --> 00:37:48.881
<v Tutor>Oxygenation: pulse oximetry, with its limits.

554
00:37:48.881 --> 00:37:54.043
<v Tutor>These are poor perfusion, carboxyhaemoglobin, methaemoglobin and skin pigmentation.

555
00:37:54.043 --> 00:38:00.170
<v Tutor>Oximetry overestimates saturation in patients with darker skin, so occult hypoxaemia is more common.

556
00:38:00.170 --> 00:38:04.708
<v Tutor>Then the arterial gas, the P F ratio and the alveolar-arterial gradient.

557
00:38:04.708 --> 00:38:08.736
<v Tutor>Ventilation: waveform capnography, and the dead space fraction.

558
00:38:08.736 --> 00:38:13.501
<v Tutor>In early A R D S, a higher dead space fraction independently predicts death.

559
00:38:13.501 --> 00:38:21.557
<v Tutor>Mechanics: plateau pressure at 30 centimetres of water or less, and driving pressure, which is plateau minus PEEP, ideally 15 or less.

560
00:38:21.557 --> 00:38:29.215
<v Tutor>In the 2015 analysis by Amato and colleagues, driving pressure was the ventilation variable most strongly linked with survival.

561
00:38:29.215 --> 00:38:35.569
<v Tutor>Add compliance, the flow and pressure waveforms, and the auto-PEEP measured with an end-expiratory hold.

562
00:38:35.569 --> 00:38:42.376
<v Tutor>Effort: P zero point one, the occlusion pressure, and oesophageal manometry to estimate transpulmonary pressure.

563
00:38:42.376 --> 00:38:48.560
<v Tutor>A P zero point one above about three and a half centimetres of water suggests high respiratory drive.

564
00:38:48.740 --> 00:38:51.460
<v Tutor>That's a warning sign of self-inflicted lung injury.

565
00:38:51.860 --> 00:38:56.564
<v Tutor>Capnography deserves its own sentence, because it's a favourite data question.

566
00:38:56.564 --> 00:39:02.746
<v Tutor>A normal trace has a flat baseline at zero, a sharp upstroke, an alveolar plateau and a sharp downstroke.

567
00:39:02.746 --> 00:39:06.575
<v Tutor>A sloping shark-fin plateau means bronchospasm or obstruction.

568
00:39:06.575 --> 00:39:12.646
<v Tutor>A baseline that doesn't return to zero means rebreathing, from an exhausted absorber or a faulty valve.

569
00:39:12.646 --> 00:39:17.679
<v Tutor>A sudden fall to zero means disconnection, oesophageal intubation or cardiac arrest.

570
00:39:17.679 --> 00:39:23.532
<v Tutor>A sudden fall to a low value suggests a big drop in cardiac output or a massive pulmonary embolism.

571
00:39:23.532 --> 00:39:29.823
<v Tutor>Small dips in the plateau, sometimes called a curare cleft, mean the patient is making spontaneous efforts.

572
00:39:29.823 --> 00:39:32.612
<v Tutor>And remember the arterial to end-tidal gap.

573
00:39:32.612 --> 00:39:35.730
<v Tutor>It normally runs at a few millimetres of mercury.

574
00:39:35.730 --> 00:39:40.380
<v Tutor>It widens with dead space, so it's a crude dead space monitor at the bedside.

575
00:39:40.780 --> 00:39:45.353
<v Tutor>Third, severe and multiple trauma, with about two dozen past questions.

576
00:39:45.353 --> 00:39:54.674
<v Tutor>Name the governing guideline: the European guideline on management of major bleeding and coagulopathy following trauma, sixth edition, published in 2023.

577
00:39:54.674 --> 00:39:56.642
<v Tutor>Its key numbers are these.

578
00:39:56.642 --> 00:40:03.705
<v Tutor>Tranexamic acid, 1 gram over 10 minutes and then 1 gram over 8 hours, within three hours of injury, as in CRASH-2.

579
00:40:03.705 --> 00:40:12.389
<v Tutor>Permissive hypotension, with a target systolic of 80 to 90 millimetres of mercury until bleeding is controlled, unless there's a brain injury.

580
00:40:12.389 --> 00:40:17.021
<v Tutor>With severe brain injury, keep the mean arterial pressure at 80 or more.

581
00:40:17.021 --> 00:40:18.699
<v Tutor>Restrict crystalloid.

582
00:40:18.699 --> 00:40:25.936
<v Tutor>Use plasma to red cells in a ratio of at least one to two, or use factor concentrates guided by viscoelastic testing.

583
00:40:25.936 --> 00:40:31.030
<v Tutor>Give fibrinogen when it's below 1.5 grams per litre, starting with 3 to 4 grams.

584
00:40:31.030 --> 00:40:34.041
<v Tutor>Target haemoglobin 70 to 90 grams per litre.

585
00:40:34.041 --> 00:40:36.125
<v Tutor>Keep ionised calcium normal.

586
00:40:36.125 --> 00:40:39.946
<v Tutor>And use damage control surgery or angioembolisation early.

587
00:40:39.946 --> 00:40:52.740
<v Tutor>The structure the College rewards is a primary survey with simultaneous resuscitation, then haemorrhage control, then the lethal triad of acidosis, hypothermia and coagulopathy, with hypocalcaemia now added to it.

588
00:40:53.140 --> 00:40:55.631
<v Tutor>Two ICU-specific angles come up often.

589
00:40:55.631 --> 00:40:57.635
<v Tutor>The first is tertiary survey.

590
00:40:57.635 --> 00:41:09.009
<v Tutor>Missed injuries are found in a meaningful minority of trauma patients after the first day, so a structured head-to-toe re-examination at twenty-four hours, with a review of all imaging, is worth a mark.

591
00:41:09.009 --> 00:41:11.825
<v Tutor>The second is the timing of definitive care.

592
00:41:11.825 --> 00:41:15.075
<v Tutor>Damage control resuscitation and surgery come first.

593
00:41:15.075 --> 00:41:21.249
<v Tutor>The patient comes to ICU to be warmed, to have coagulopathy and acidosis corrected and to be resuscitated.

594
00:41:21.249 --> 00:41:26.773
<v Tutor>Then they go back for definitive repair once physiology allows, typically within a day or two.

595
00:41:26.773 --> 00:41:35.060
<v Tutor>Add venous thromboembolism prophylaxis once bleeding is controlled, usually within twenty-four to forty-eight hours, and early enteral nutrition.

596
00:41:35.460 --> 00:41:36.895
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

597
00:41:36.895 --> 00:41:41.420
<v Tutor>What's the dose and timing of dexamethasone in adult bacterial meningitis?

598
00:41:46.820 --> 00:41:54.019
<v Tutor>Ten milligrams intravenously every six hours for four days, started just before or with the first antibiotic dose.

599
00:41:54.019 --> 00:41:59.801
<v Tutor>That's our fourth item, meningitis and encephalitis, with more than twenty past questions.

600
00:41:59.801 --> 00:42:05.466
<v Tutor>The evidence is de Gans and van de Beek, in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2002.

601
00:42:05.466 --> 00:42:08.357
<v Tutor>It included three hundred and one adults.

602
00:42:08.357 --> 00:42:15.556
<v Tutor>Dexamethasone reduced unfavourable outcome, with a relative risk of 0.59, and death, with a relative risk of 0.48.

603
00:42:15.556 --> 00:42:22.577
<v Tutor>The benefit was concentrated in pneumococcal meningitis, where unfavourable outcome fell from 52 to 26 percent.

604
00:42:22.577 --> 00:42:27.239
<v Tutor>The 2016 ESCMID guideline recommends ceftriaxone 2 grams twelve-hourly.

605
00:42:27.239 --> 00:42:36.503
<v Tutor>Add vancomycin where pneumococcal resistance is a concern, and amoxicillin or benzylpenicillin for Listeria in those over fifty or immunocompromised.

606
00:42:36.503 --> 00:42:38.922
<v Tutor>Don't let a CT delay antibiotics.

607
00:42:38.922 --> 00:42:46.239
<v Tutor>Scan first only if there's a focal deficit, new seizures, a Glasgow Coma Scale below 10, or severe immunocompromise.

608
00:42:46.239 --> 00:42:51.077
<v Tutor>For encephalitis, start aciclovir 10 milligrams per kilogram eight-hourly.

609
00:42:51.077 --> 00:43:01.580
<v Tutor>Send cerebrospinal fluid for herpes simplex PCR, get an MRI looking at the temporal lobes, and remember autoimmune encephalitis, especially anti-NMDA receptor antibodies.

610
00:43:01.980 --> 00:43:05.832
<v Tutor>For the data question, know the cerebrospinal fluid patterns.

611
00:43:05.832 --> 00:43:17.613
<v Tutor>Bacterial meningitis: a raised opening pressure, a neutrophil-predominant white cell count often in the thousands, a high protein, and a cerebrospinal fluid to blood glucose ratio below about point four.

612
00:43:17.613 --> 00:43:23.921
<v Tutor>Viral: a lymphocytic count in the tens to hundreds, a modestly raised protein and a normal glucose ratio.

613
00:43:23.921 --> 00:43:31.403
<v Tutor>Tuberculous or fungal: lymphocytes, a very high protein and a low glucose, often with cranial nerve palsies and hydrocephalus.

614
00:43:31.403 --> 00:43:37.935
<v Tutor>And for cryptococcus, think of a very high opening pressure that needs repeated therapeutic lumbar punctures.

615
00:43:37.935 --> 00:43:40.670
<v Tutor>Two public health points carry marks too.

616
00:43:40.670 --> 00:43:49.380
<v Tutor>Notify the public health unit for meningococcal disease, and arrange clearance antibiotics for close contacts, usually ciprofloxacin or ceftriaxone.

617
00:43:49.780 --> 00:43:54.346
<v Tutor>Fifth, intubation of the critically ill, with more than twenty past questions.

618
00:43:54.346 --> 00:44:02.736
<v Tutor>Name the Difficult Airway Society guideline for tracheal intubation in critically ill adults, published in the British Journal of Anaesthesia in 2018.

619
00:44:02.736 --> 00:44:04.329
<v Tutor>Its pillars are these.

620
00:44:04.329 --> 00:44:06.665
<v Tutor>Plan for failure, with plans A to D.

621
00:44:06.665 --> 00:44:11.975
<v Tutor>Preoxygenate with non-invasive ventilation or high-flow nasal oxygen in hypoxaemic patients.

622
00:44:11.975 --> 00:44:14.842
<v Tutor>Use waveform capnography for every intubation.

623
00:44:14.842 --> 00:44:16.063
<v Tutor>Limit attempts.

624
00:44:16.063 --> 00:44:22.435
<v Tutor>And move to front-of-neck access with a scalpel, bougie and tube in a can't intubate, can't oxygenate situation.

625
00:44:22.435 --> 00:44:28.382
<v Tutor>The trial to quote is DEVICE, by Prekker and colleagues, in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2023.

626
00:44:28.382 --> 00:44:34.170
<v Tutor>It randomised fourteen hundred and twenty critically ill adults in US emergency departments and ICUs.

627
00:44:34.170 --> 00:44:40.435
<v Tutor>First-attempt success was 85.1 percent with video laryngoscopy, against 70.8 percent with direct laryngoscopy.

628
00:44:40.435 --> 00:44:42.665
<v Tutor>Severe complications were similar.

629
00:44:42.665 --> 00:44:46.594
<v Tutor>The other major hazard is peri-intubation cardiovascular collapse.

630
00:44:46.594 --> 00:44:52.860
<v Tutor>So resuscitate first, choose induction agents and doses carefully, and have a vasopressor running or drawn up.

631
00:44:53.260 --> 00:44:56.385
<v Tutor>Pick the induction agent with the patient in mind.

632
00:44:56.385 --> 00:45:04.682
<v Tutor>Ketamine, about 1 to 1.5 milligrams per kilogram, or a reduced dose of propofol, with rocuronium at 1.2 milligrams per kilogram for a quick onset.

633
00:45:04.682 --> 00:45:06.514
<v Tutor>Keep sugammadex available.

634
00:45:06.514 --> 00:45:10.177
<v Tutor>Position the patient head-up or ramped, especially if obese.

635
00:45:10.177 --> 00:45:15.242
<v Tutor>Before induction, correct hypoxaemia, hypovolaemia and acidosis as far as time allows.

636
00:45:15.242 --> 00:45:17.828
<v Tutor>A severe metabolic acidosis is the trap.

637
00:45:17.828 --> 00:45:20.629
<v Tutor>The patient is breathing hard to compensate.

638
00:45:20.629 --> 00:45:27.795
<v Tutor>If you lose that compensation during apnoea, or set a normal minute ventilation afterwards, the pH can fall catastrophically.

639
00:45:27.795 --> 00:45:31.620
<v Tutor>So match the minute ventilation to their pre-intubation effort.

640
00:45:32.020 --> 00:45:36.669
<v Tutor>Sixth, chronic hepatic failure, with more than twenty past questions.

641
00:45:36.669 --> 00:45:38.118
<v Tutor>Know the scores.

642
00:45:38.118 --> 00:45:43.068
<v Tutor>Child-Pugh uses bilirubin, albumin, the I N R, ascites and encephalopathy.

643
00:45:43.068 --> 00:45:47.838
<v Tutor>MELD uses bilirubin, I N R and creatinine, and MELD-sodium adds sodium.

644
00:45:47.838 --> 00:45:50.494
<v Tutor>Then acute-on-chronic liver failure.

645
00:45:50.494 --> 00:45:54.962
<v Tutor>The 2023 EASL guideline grades it by the number of organ failures.

646
00:45:54.962 --> 00:46:02.026
<v Tutor>Twenty-eight-day mortality rises from roughly one in five at grade one to about three in four at grade three.

647
00:46:02.026 --> 00:46:09.089
<v Tutor>Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis is diagnosed by an ascitic neutrophil count above 250 per cubic millimetre.

648
00:46:09.089 --> 00:46:16.998
<v Tutor>Treat it with antibiotics plus albumin, 1.5 grams per kilogram in the first six hours and 1 gram per kilogram on day three.

649
00:46:16.998 --> 00:46:19.896
<v Tutor>That's from Sort and colleagues in 1999.

650
00:46:19.896 --> 00:46:25.994
<v Tutor>Renal impairment fell from 33 to 10 percent, and in-hospital mortality from 29 to 10 percent.

651
00:46:25.994 --> 00:46:30.522
<v Tutor>For hepatorenal acute kidney injury, use terlipressin with albumin.

652
00:46:30.522 --> 00:46:36.619
<v Tutor>In the CONFIRM trial, terlipressin roughly doubled the rate of hepatorenal syndrome reversal.

653
00:46:36.619 --> 00:46:42.415
<v Tutor>But it increased respiratory failure, so assess volume status and oxygenation carefully.

654
00:46:42.415 --> 00:46:49.660
<v Tutor>And after a large-volume paracentesis of more than five litres, give about 8 grams of albumin per litre removed.

655
00:46:50.060 --> 00:46:55.266
<v Tutor>The structure for a chronic liver failure question in the ICU runs system by system.

656
00:46:55.266 --> 00:47:07.601
<v Tutor>Neurological: hepatic encephalopathy, graded by West Haven, treated with lactulose and rifaximin, with a search for a precipitant such as bleeding, infection, constipation, sedatives or electrolyte disturbance.

657
00:47:07.601 --> 00:47:14.392
<v Tutor>Cardiovascular: low systemic vascular resistance and a high cardiac output, with relative adrenal insufficiency.

658
00:47:14.392 --> 00:47:19.994
<v Tutor>Respiratory: hepatopulmonary syndrome, portopulmonary hypertension and hepatic hydrothorax.

659
00:47:19.994 --> 00:47:29.726
<v Tutor>Gastrointestinal: variceal bleeding, treated with terlipressin, antibiotics, endoscopic banding and, if that fails, a transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt.

660
00:47:29.726 --> 00:47:32.386
<v Tutor>Haematological: rebalanced haemostasis.

661
00:47:32.386 --> 00:47:39.459
<v Tutor>A high I N R doesn't mean the patient is anticoagulated, so don't give plasma to correct a number before a procedure.

662
00:47:39.459 --> 00:47:45.740
<v Tutor>And always ask whether the patient is a transplant candidate, because that changes the ceiling of care.

663
00:47:47.140 --> 00:47:49.500
<v Tutor>That's the spiral for this week.

664
00:47:49.500 --> 00:47:51.742
<v Tutor>One thing to do before Monday.

665
00:47:51.742 --> 00:48:01.476
<v Tutor>After today's set, take the pyroglutamic acidosis question and write part d from memory in four lines: glutathione, the brake, the drain, and flucloxacillin.

666
00:48:01.476 --> 00:48:09.500
<v Tutor>Then write the S M R investigation as four headings: is it real, is the data right, is it case mix or the model, and is it care.

667
00:48:09.500 --> 00:48:12.509
<v Tutor>Check both against today's College answers.

668
00:48:12.509 --> 00:48:14.220
<v Tutor>Thanks for listening.
